WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.392
SQL injection prevention in Go starts with
one central habit:

2
00:00:03.472 --> 00:00:05.808
keep SQL structure separate from runtime
values.

3
00:00:05.888 --> 00:00:08.558
Trusted application code should choose the
query shape.

4
00:00:08.638 --> 00:00:12.142
Runtime values should be supplied through
parameters or bound arguments.

5
00:00:12.222 --> 00:00:15.003
That separation should be visible in
ordinary code review,

6
00:00:15.083 --> 00:00:17.308
not hidden in a special security-only
process.

7
00:00:17.388 --> 00:00:19.835
Dynamic query behavior needs a similar
discipline.

8
00:00:19.915 --> 00:00:21.361
Values remain parameterized.

9
00:00:21.441 --> 00:00:24.278
Structure such as sort fields, report
modes, table choices,

10
00:00:24.358 --> 00:00:27.306
and selected columns should come from
controlled allowlists.

11
00:00:27.386 --> 00:00:31.335
Optional filters should be assembled from
known fragments selected by application

12
00:00:31.415 --> 00:00:31.748
logic.

13
00:00:31.828 --> 00:00:35.444
Search and pagination should have clear
limits so database behavior remains

14
00:00:35.524 --> 00:00:37.081
predictable under real traffic.

15
00:00:37.161 --> 00:00:40.387
Validation and normalization make the
boundary easier to maintain.

16
00:00:40.467 --> 00:00:42.580
Decode external data into expected Go
types.

17
00:00:42.660 --> 00:00:44.830
Convert request shapes into internal
models.

18
00:00:44.910 --> 00:00:47.913
Reject unsupported choices before query
logic depends on them.

19
00:00:47.993 --> 00:00:49.550
Then still parameterize values.

20
00:00:49.630 --> 00:00:52.133
Validation decides whether a request is
meaningful;

21
00:00:52.213 --> 00:00:55.217
parameterization preserves how the
database receives values.

22
00:00:55.297 --> 00:00:58.467
Abstractions, permissions, and operations
complete the picture.

23
00:00:58.547 --> 00:01:02.274
ORMs, query builders, generated query
layers, and stored procedures can help,

24
00:01:02.354 --> 00:01:06.024
but teams must understand their safe paths
and review their lower-level entry

25
00:01:06.104 --> 00:01:06.494
points.

26
00:01:06.574 --> 00:01:10.133
Database accounts should use least
privilege so mistakes have less impact.

27
00:01:10.213 --> 00:01:12.383
Logging, error handling, tests,
monitoring,

28
00:01:12.463 --> 00:01:14.632
and review should reinforce the same
pattern.

29
00:01:14.712 --> 00:01:17.048
Secure Go SQL access is not a single
function call.

30
00:01:17.128 --> 00:01:20.020
It is a set of habits that make safe query
behavior repeatable.
